          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            One of the main tenets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            is that in general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joint+and+marginal+probability"} the probability of the product is not the product of the probabilities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation"} correlation
            and other
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+interactions"} statistical interactions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+tensor+product"} infinite product
            is not the infinite product of the probabilities.
          %p
            However, an infinite product is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofiltered+limit"} cofiltered limit
            of finite products, and under some conditions,
            = succeed "." do
              %em the probability of an infinite product is the limit of the probability of the finite products
          %p
            Statements of this kind are known collectively as the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Kolmogorov extension theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+processes"} stochastic processes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joint+distribution"} joint distribution
            over an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+tensor+product"} infinite product
          %p
            In terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category-theoretic+approaches+to+probability+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserved
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meas"} measurable spaces
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Markov kernels
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category#Tlim"} equivalently
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
          %h2#in_measuretheoretic_probability In measure-theoretic probability
          %p We look at the classical statement, and then give two (related) ways to express it category-theoretically.
          %h3#classical_statement Classical statement
          %p
            The classical statement is about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+tensor+product"} infinite product
            is uniquely determined by its finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marginal+distribution"} marginals
          %p
          #kolmogorov.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(X_j,\mathcal{A}_j)\}_{j\in J}
              be a collection of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+Borel+spaces"} standard Borel spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              of arbitrary cardinality.
            %ul
              %li
                Denote by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi J
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_J,\mathcal{A}_J)
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_j
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} sigma-algebra
              %li
                Similarly, for every finite set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi J
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\subseteq J
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_F,\mathcal{A}_F)
                the product of the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_j,\mathcal{A}_j)
                for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in F
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              between
            %ul
              %li
                probability measures on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi J
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_J,\mathcal{A}_J)
            %p and
            %ul
              %li
                families of probability measures
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_F
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_F,\mathcal{A}_F)
                for each finite subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\subseteq J
                such that for every inclusion
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\subseteq G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_F
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marginal+distribution"} marginal
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_G
            %p
              In particular, the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_F
              can be obtained as marginal distributions of the measure
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_J
            %p
          %p Note that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The same result holds more in general if each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_j,\mathcal{A}_j)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
                equipped with its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sigma-algebra"} Borel sigma-algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_F
                are
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    inner regular
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/inner+regular+measure"} ?
            %li
              %p The uncountable product of standard Borel spaces may fail to be standard Borel.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sigma-algebra"} Borel sigma-algebra
                of an uncountable product of topological spaces is not the product of the Borel sigma-algebras of the factors.
          %h3#in_the_category_of_markov_kernels In the category of Markov kernels
          %p
            We can restate Kolmogorov’s extension theorem as a
